    "updated_at": "2019-09-16T04:27:32Z",
    "body": "This seems related to #279, in that it's a problem with the shell interface and starship.\r\n\r\nThe problem here is that zle reset-prompt seems to redraw the `PROMPT` variable without executing any of the precmd/preexec hooks.\r\n\r\nIn most shell frameworks, this isn't a problem, because the prompt contains special characters (e.g `%d`) that are evaluated in the prompt-drawing function itself. Unfortunately, since `starship` sets up an external binary to draw the prompt with, and doesn't actually use any prompt variables, there's no change to be redrawn.\r\n\r\nNow, there's a way around this for the time being: you can change your `foo` function to the following:\r\n\r\n```\r\nfunction foo {\r\n    cd ..\r\n    starship_precmd\r\n    zle reset-prompt\r\n}\r\n```\r\nThis will cause the pwd to change correctly, but it'll also have the unfortunate side effect of breaking the vim mode indicator (if you're using vim-mode).\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, due to the way `reset-prompt` works, I don't think we can make this work in an automated fashion (i.e. automatically trigger starship_precmd when reset-prompt is called). If anyone has any ideas, feel free to throw them out!",
